At the login endpoint, after `AuthenticationManager` validates credentials, generate a signed JWT with the user's identity and authorities as claims.

```java
public String generateToken(Authentication authentication) {
    Instant now = Instant.now();
    List<String> roles = authentication.getAuthorities().stream()
        .map(GrantedAuthority::getAuthority)
        .toList();

    return Jwts.builder()
        .subject(authentication.getName())       // user identifier
        .claim("roles", roles)                    // authorities for authorization
        .issuedAt(Date.from(now))
        .expiration(Date.from(now.plus(15, ChronoUnit.MINUTES))) // short-lived
        .signWith(secretKey)                      // HS256 with injected key
        .compact();
}
```

**15–30 minutes is the production standard for access token expiry.** A stolen token is usable until expiry — there is no way to invalidate a valid JWT without a blocklist. Short expiry limits the damage window. Pair short-lived access tokens with a **refresh token** (hours or days, stored in an HttpOnly cookie, rotated on each use) to maintain sessions without frequent re-authentication. The refresh token can be stored server-side and validated against a database — this is acceptable because refresh is an infrequent operation.

**Never put sensitive data in JWT claims.** The payload is Base64URL-encoded, not encrypted. Anyone who holds the token can decode the payload without the secret key. Store only identifiers and roles — never passwords, PII, or internal system IDs that reveal infrastructure details.

For signing algorithm selection: HS256 uses one shared secret (simpler, works for single services). RS256 uses a private key for signing and a public key for verification (the public key can be distributed to all resource servers without exposing the signing capability). For any microservice architecture, prefer RS256 — it eliminates secret distribution.

The filter runs on every request, extracts the Bearer token, validates it, and sets the authentication in the security context. Extending `OncePerRequestFilter` guarantees single execution per request even if the filter chain re-dispatches.

```java
@Override
protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request,
                                HttpServletResponse response,
                                FilterChain chain) throws IOException, ServletException {
    String authHeader = request.getHeader("Authorization");
    if (authHeader == null || !authHeader.startsWith("Bearer ")) {
        chain.doFilter(request, response); // no token — let security chain handle it
        return;
    }

    String token = authHeader.substring(7);
    Claims claims = jwtUtil.validateAndParse(token); // throws SignatureException or ExpiredJwtException

    List<GrantedAuthority> authorities = ((List<String>) claims.get("roles")).stream()
        .map(SimpleGrantedAuthority::new)
        .toList();

    // credential is null — the signed token IS the proof, credentials not needed after auth
    UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken auth =
        new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(claims.getSubject(), null, authorities);

    SecurityContextHolder.getContext().setAuthentication(auth);
    chain.doFilter(request, response);
}
```

**The filter does not call `loadUserByUsername()`** — that would be a database hit on every request. The JWT carries the authorities directly as claims. The tradeoff is that role changes don't take effect until the current token expires. If you need immediate role propagation, you must either use very short token expiry or add a token version check against the database (reintroducing the round-trip).

**Do not catch JWT exceptions in the filter.** Let `SignatureException` and `ExpiredJwtException` propagate. `ExceptionTranslationFilter` catches them and sends a 401 response with the appropriate headers. Catching them and calling `chain.doFilter()` with an empty security context means the request continues unauthenticated — a security bypass.
